WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.733 --> 00:00:06.614
Every Linux program starts with three streams already open.

2
00:00:06.614 --> 00:00:08.784
A stream is just a flow of bytes.

3
00:00:08.933 --> 00:00:10.415
Stream zero is stdin.

4
00:00:10.415 --> 00:00:13.167
The program reads its input from there.

5
00:00:13.167 --> 00:00:15.567
By default, that is your keyboard.

6
00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:17.188
Stream one is stdout.

7
00:00:17.188 --> 00:00:19.751
The program writes its results there.

8
00:00:19.751 --> 00:00:22.175
By default, they go to your screen.

9
00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:25.209
Stream two is stderr, for error messages.

10
00:00:25.209 --> 00:00:28.085
By default, that goes to your screen too.

11
00:00:28.267 --> 00:00:31.279
Zero, one and two are called file descriptors.

12
00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:35.405
They are the small numbers a program uses for its open streams.

13
00:00:35.600 --> 00:00:38.218
The program does not open these three itself.

14
00:00:38.218 --> 00:00:41.011
It inherits them from the shell that started it.

15
00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:43.614
The program just writes to stream one.

16
00:00:43.614 --> 00:00:48.061
It does not know if one leads to a screen, a file, or another program.

17
00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:54.498
So the shell can point a stream somewhere else before the program starts, and the program needs no change.

18
00:00:54.498 --> 00:00:56.875
Redirection and pipes both rely on this.

19
00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:58.401
Let's try it.

20
00:00:58.401 --> 00:01:00.096
Run ls app.log nosuch.

21
00:01:00.096 --> 00:01:03.948
One of those files exists, and the other does not.

22
00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.844
You get one result line and one error line.

23
00:01:06.844 --> 00:01:10.248
On screen they look alike, but they travel separately.

24
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.160
Put a greater-than sign and a file name after the command.

25
00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:18.051
Before ls even starts, the shell points stdout at that file.

26
00:01:18.200 --> 00:01:20.039
The result goes into the file.

27
00:01:20.039 --> 00:01:24.208
The error still lands on your screen, because only stream one moved.

28
00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:27.636
One greater-than sign replaces what was in the file.

29
00:01:27.636 --> 00:01:31.432
Two of them add to the end, so a second run leaves two lines.

30
00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:36.298
To move stream two instead, put a two right before the greater-than sign.

31
00:01:36.298 --> 00:01:41.704
Here it goes to /dev/null, a special file that throws away everything written to it.

32
00:01:41.867 --> 00:01:42.649
Run it now.

33
00:01:42.649 --> 00:01:47.057
The result replaces the file, and the error simply disappears.

34
00:01:47.057 --> 00:01:48.692
The screen stays empty.

35
00:01:48.867 --> 00:01:51.695
Or write two, greater-than, ampersand, one.

36
00:01:51.695 --> 00:01:54.917
Read it as: send two wherever one goes right now.

37
00:01:55.067 --> 00:01:59.980
Now both lines land in out.txt, and nothing reaches the screen.

38
00:02:00.933 --> 00:02:01.729
Now, pipes.

39
00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:08.456
A pipe, written as a vertical bar, connects one program's stdout to the next program's stdin.

40
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:11.713
Take grep ERROR app.log, piped into wc -l.

41
00:02:11.713 --> 00:02:18.533
First, the shell creates the pipe, a small buffer that the operating system keeps in memory.

42
00:02:18.733 --> 00:02:23.081
It points grep's stdout at one end, and wc's stdin at the other.

43
00:02:23.081 --> 00:02:26.341
Then it starts both programs at the same moment.

44
00:02:26.533 --> 00:02:27.757
Here is app.log.

45
00:02:27.757 --> 00:02:30.664
It has thirty-seven lines, like these.

46
00:02:30.800 --> 00:02:36.412
grep reads the lines one at a time, and passes on only the lines that contain ERROR.

47
00:02:36.412 --> 00:02:38.216
The other lines stop there.

48
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:40.860
wc counts each line as it arrives.

49
00:02:40.860 --> 00:02:43.957
Lines keep flowing while both programs work.

50
00:02:43.957 --> 00:02:46.209
Nobody waits for the whole file.

51
00:02:46.467 --> 00:02:52.085
When the input ends, wc prints its count to its own stdout, your screen.

52
00:02:52.085 --> 00:02:52.475
Four.

53
00:02:52.667 --> 00:02:55.409
Neither program knows the other exists.

54
00:02:55.409 --> 00:03:00.893
grep writes to stream one, wc reads stream zero, and the shell joined the two.

55
00:03:01.067 --> 00:03:03.077
Only stdout goes into a pipe.

56
00:03:03.077 --> 00:03:08.483
Error messages leave on stderr, skip the pipe, and go straight to your screen.

57
00:03:08.667 --> 00:03:10.281
Think of an assembly line.

58
00:03:10.281 --> 00:03:14.752
Parts move along the belt one at a time, and every worker works at once.

59
00:03:14.752 --> 00:03:19.161
Broken parts leave by a side door, and the next worker never sees them.

60
00:03:19.333 --> 00:03:23.544
Every program gets stdin, stdout and stderr from the shell.

61
00:03:23.544 --> 00:03:30.611
A greater-than sign points a stream at a file, and a pipe joins stdout to the next program's stdin.
